---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import LoopFlow from '../../components/diagrams/LoopFlow.astro';
import '../../styles/story.css';
---
<Base wide title="The maintainer loop — an AI that maintains my plugin and has to ask me first" description="How an AI loop maintains my WordPress plugin: it reads the forum and writes fixes, plain code checks the results, and nothing goes out until I approve that one action from my phone.">
<div class="story">

  <section class="beat" style="border-top:0">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">The maintainer loop</p>
      <h1>An AI maintains my plugin. It has to ask me first.</h1>
      <p class="big">An AI reads the support forum, writes fixes, drafts replies and prepares releases
      for <a href="/projects/gdpr-recaptcha/">a WordPress plugin with about 4,000 installs</a>. Then it stops and sends me a message.
      I answer with one word, "ja", and exactly that one thing happens. Nothing else.</p>
      <div class="card shot"><LoopFlow /></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat alt">
    <div class="in split">
      <div><p class="kicker">The main question</p><h2>How much should an AI agent be allowed to do?</h2></div>
      <div>
        <p class="big">Enough to do real work, but not enough to do damage on its own.</p>
        <p class="muted">My answer is a strict split. The model may propose anything. Deciding and executing happen in
        plain code, and every step that can't be undone needs a human, every time.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">Four roles</p>
      <h2>How the work is split.</h2>
      <p class="big">The parts only talk to each other through files. There is no shared memory and nothing hidden.</p>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><div><h3>Scout <span class="who rules">rules</span></h3><p>Checks the plugin's forum and reviews, competitors' changelogs, form-builder forums and its own live spam data. Anything new goes into an inbox. No AI involved.</p></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Worker <span class="who ai">AI</span></h3><p>An AI coding session that runs unattended and implements one item on its own branch. Then a verifier in plain code, not the AI, decides whether the result may be proposed at all.</p></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Concierge <span class="who ai">AI</span> + <span class="who rules" style="margin-left:0">rules</span></h3><p>Talks to me over Signal. The model may word the messages and suggest "go" or "veto" for one existing item, but it can never execute anything.</p></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Gate <span class="who rules">rules</span></h3><p>The only part that can act on the outside world: releases, merges, forum posts. One approval per action, tied to my phone number. An approval never carries over to the next action.</p></div></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat alt">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">Untrusted input</p>
      <h2>I assume someone will try to give the AI instructions.</h2>
      <p class="big">Forum posts are written by strangers, and some of them may try to tell an AI what to do.</p>
      <div class="grid3">
        <div><h3>Outside text is fenced off</h3><p>Anything from outside reaches the model clearly marked as untrusted data, never as instructions.</p></div>
        <div><h3>The approval question isn't AI-written</h3><p>The text of what I approve comes from fixed templates. No model and no forum post can change the wording of the question I answer with "ja".</p></div>
        <div><h3>One question at a time</h3><p>Only one confirmation can be open, and only for a short time. If a new notification arrives while a question is open, the old question is cancelled, so a "ja" can't land on the wrong thing.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Releases need a different word</h3><p>A release to WordPress.org can't be confirmed with the word that approves a merge. The risk is different, so the word is too.</p></div>
        <div><h3>No reminders</h3><p>A pending release shows up in the daily summary, but there is no reminder that gets more urgent, because the pace of changes is partly driven by text from strangers.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Fails closed</h3><p>If the stored state can't be read, the model is broken or a marker is missing, the loop always takes the safe option.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat">
    <div class="in split">
      <div><p class="kicker">From the phone</p><h2>Releasing by text message.</h2></div>
      <div>
        <p class="big">I type "release 5.2.0". The loop prepares everything in an isolated copy: version bump,
        changelog from the commit history, validation. Then it shows me what will go out and waits.</p>
        <p class="muted">An AI may improve the English of the changelog once, if I want. The raw text is shown right next to it,
        so I compare the two instead of trusting a rewrite. If the model doesn't answer, the release still goes out with the
        raw text. A model never blocks a release.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat alt">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">Monitoring</p>
      <h2>"Not measured" is not the same as "fine".</h2>
      <p class="big">Small test runs, so-called canaries, check every day that the AI's sandbox still holds and that the model still
      behaves as expected. When a check can't run, it says so. It never reports "healthy" for something it didn't test.</p>
      <p class="muted">Something I learned in practice: a loop that reports its own failures correctly still needs a second channel
      to reach me. In agent systems, a lot of the real engineering is not in the agent but in the monitoring around it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">By the numbers</p>
      <h2>How big it is.</h2>
      <div class="numbers">
        <div><strong>~30,000</strong><span>lines of loop code across almost 100 modules</span></div>
        <div><strong>1,889</strong><span>automated tests; the test runner refuses any real model call</span></div>
        <div><strong>4</strong><span>roles: scout, worker, concierge, gate</span></div>
        <div><strong>1 word</strong><span>from me before a prepared change goes out</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat alt">
    <div class="in read">
      <p class="kicker">The general idea</p>
      <h2>The same split works elsewhere.</h2>
      <p class="big">The model proposes, plain code decides what is possible at all, and a human approves every step that
      can't be undone. I use it for a WordPress plugin, but it fits anything where an AI should act, just not alone.</p>
      <a class="go" href="mailto:matthias.nordwig@programmiere.de?subject=Maintainer%20loop">Email me about it</a>
    </div>
  </section>

</div>
</Base>
